Binary → Octal

Group every 3 bits into one octal digit, from Right to Left.

(110101010)₂
 ↓   ↓   ↓
 1   5   2   →  (152)₈ (wait: shown in notes as 159 with grouping 1|5|9)
Example from notes:
(110101010)₂
 ↓   ↓   ↓
 1   5   9   →  (159)₈  (approx — note grouping R to L)

Hexadecimal → Binary

Ungroup each hex digit into 4 binary bits.

(6x32)₁₆  i.e., 6, 3, 2
  ↓   ↓
8010  0011   →  binary equivalent

Binary → Hexadecimal

Group every 4 binary bits into one hex digit, from Right to Left.

Example:
(1011 0110 1010)₂
  ↓    ↓    ↓
  B    6    A    →  (B6A)₁₆

⚠️ Not Possible Directly
Octal ←→ Hexadecimal  (Direct conversion NOT possible)
Must convert through Binary as intermediate:
Octal → Binary → Hexadecimal
Hexadecimal → Binary → Octal

2. Format Specifiers
SpecifierMeaning%dInteger (signed)%iInteger%cCharacter%oOctal%xHexadecimal (lower)%XHexadecimal (upper)%fFloat (IEEE 754)
Key Points

Except floating-point values, all remaining data is internally stored in binary representation.
Format specifiers like %d, %i, %c, %o, %x, %X are designed to print/format binary data in respective formats.

e.g., %o takes binary data as input and formats it into octal format.


Format specifiers are meant for formatting data from one form to another — not for converting.

Floating-Point Note

All floating values are internally stored in a special representation called IEEE 754 (Standard Floating Point Representation). %f is designed to print these data.


Example — Multiple Format Specifiers
cvoid main() {
    printf("%d %i %c %o %x %X", 135, 135, 135, 135, 135);
}
Output:
135   135   207   87   87
